course: dictionary
lesson: 13815
title: Damage that cannot be mended
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «The oil spill irreparably harmed the coral reef.» Irreparably means in a way that cannot be repaired, mended or put right.
explain: Break the word into parts. Ir- means not, repair means to fix, and -ly tells you how something happened. So something that is irreparably damaged cannot be fixed again.
explain: Use this word for serious harm, such as a habitat, a friendship or a vase. «Mum's favourite vase fell and was irreparably broken.» The word broken is a strong clue: the damage is so bad that no one can put it right.
fact: Irreparably is an adverb. The adjective is irreparable.
fact: «Irreparably» means in a way that cannot be repaired, mended or put right.
fact: It can also mean so badly that it cannot be undone.
fact: Common phrases are «irreparably damaged» and «irreparably harmed».
fact: To check your meaning, swap in «cannot be fixed». If the sentence still makes sense, you have it right.
exercise: «The oil spill irreparably harmed the coral reef.» What does irreparably mean here? | A little, and it heals quickly | Loudly and in public | * In a way that cannot be put right | Very slowly, one piece at a time
exercise: Lily says irreparable means easy to fix. What does it really mean? | Shiny and new | Easy to mend in a day | Heavy to carry | * Impossible to mend or put right
exercise: Find and copy the word that means cannot be mended in «The fire irreparably destroyed the old barn.» | old | * irreparably | barn | fire
test: «The old painting was irreparably torn down the middle.» What does irreparably tell you about the tear? | * It cannot be repaired | It is very small and neat | It was done on purpose to make a pattern | It happened only once, long ago
test: Which picture shows something irreparably damaged? | A sock with a tiny hole that can be darned | * A glass plate smashed into a hundred tiny pieces | A pencil with a small scratch that you can sharpen away | A bike with a flat tyre that a shop can fix
exam: Which sentence uses irreparably correctly? | The cake was irreparably tasty and warm. | I irreparably finished my homework before tea. | * The river was irreparably polluted, so the fish could no longer live there. | We walked irreparably to the park after lunch.
